[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62837,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Desmoid fibromatosis is a locally aggressive but non-metastasizing deep-seated (myo)fibroblastic neoplasm with infiltrative growth and a propensity for local recurrence.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62838,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8822/1 Abdominal fibromatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62839,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F74 &amp; XH6116 Neoplasms of uncertain behaviour of peritoneum &amp; Abdominal (mesenteric) fibromatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62840,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> desmoid tumour; extra-abdominal fibromatosis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62841,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62842,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours most commonly arise from the pelvic floor {10945361}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62843,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>As many as 50% of patients present with pain, whereas urinary symptoms are the chief presentation in a smaller subset of patients, and obstructed labour may occur in pregnant patients {10945361; 16343190; 22315870}. Nearly one&nbsp;third of cases are asymptomatic. The mean size is 11&nbsp;cm (range: 3&ndash;30&nbsp;cm) {10945361}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62844,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Desmoid fibromatosis is uncommon. Patients are usually women of reproductive age (mean age: 30&nbsp;years; range: 17&ndash;62&nbsp;years) {10945361}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62845,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is multifactorial, including hormonal factors (including pregnancy), physical factors (surgery) {10945361}, and genetic factors (sporadic somatic <em>CTNNB1</em> mutations and germline <em>APC</em> mutations in Gardner syndrome).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62846,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Activating mutations in <em>CTNNB1</em> (the gene that encodes &beta;-catenin) and inactivating mutations in <em>APC</em> (a tumour suppressor gene) cause decreased proteosomal degradation and increased nuclear accumulation of &beta;-catenin, which ultimately promotes cell proliferation and survival through upregulation of the WNT/&beta;-catenin pathway {19099242; 19436199; 28575679; 17952864}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62847,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours are solitary, firm, and relatively circumscribed grossly. The cut surface is whorled and white, with occasional myxoid/cystic change.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62848,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There is an infiltrative proliferation of bland fibroblasts/myofibroblasts with variable mitotic activity arranged in long sweeping fascicles. Lymphoid aggregates are often present at the advancing tumour edge. Extensive hyalinization or keloid-like collagen may be present {27124915}. The tumour cells are immunoreactive for actin, with occasional desmin staining. The vast majority of tumours show nuclear expression of &beta;-catenin {17711447; 15832090}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62849,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62850,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Because most sporadic cases harbour point mutations in <em>CTNNB1</em>, mutation analysis may be useful when morphological features are not diagnostic and &beta;-catenin immunohistochemistry is equivocal.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62851,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> fibroblasts and myofibroblasts without atypia arranged in long sweeping fascicles; infiltrative growth.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> nuclear &beta;-catenin expression.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62852,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62853,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 149,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Almost 40% of tumours recur after surgery, often within 2&nbsp;years, but the natural course is unpredictable {10945361}. Currently, conservative management (specifically with close surveillance) has been recommended, with medical treatment (antihormone or targeted therapy, chemotherapy) in cases with progression {28961825; 25315103}. Surgery may be considered in resectable cases not associated with compromised function. Some studies have suggested that <em>CTNNB1</em> (&beta;-catenin) mutation may be predictive of tumour recurrence {21323417; 23913621; 20197769; 18832571}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:16:02"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]